{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git diff --cached -- skills/frontend/PATTERNS.md skills/frontend/TESTING.md'","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/skills/frontend/PATTERNS.md b/skills/frontend/PATTERNS.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..c733845\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/skills/frontend/PATTERNS.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,155 @@\n+# Frontend Patterns\n+\n+Use these examples as reference points when applying the deep-component rules from [DEEP-COMPONENTS.md](DEEP-COMPONENTS.md).\n+\n+## Deep Component Examples\n+\n+### FilesDrawer - small prop interface owning a workflow\n+\n+Rule: Small Semantic Interfaces + Collapse Coordination\n+\n+Source: `~/Code/toyo` file management drawer\n+\n+```typescript\n+interface FilesDrawerProps {\n+  open: boolean\n+  onClose: () => void\n+  mode?: \"drawer\" | \"page\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Three props, two semantic events. The component owns file upload, delete, directory navigation, drag-drop, and folder creation internally. The parent only knows \"open/close\" and \"drawer or page mode.\"\n+\n+### BrowserPane - derives state from props\n+\n+Rule: Derive Don't Sync\n+\n+Source: `~/Code/toyo` browser preview pane\n+\n+```typescript\n+const iframeUrl = useMemo(\n+  () => buildLiveViewUrl(browserSession.liveViewUrl, readOnlyMode),\n+  [browserSession.liveViewUrl, readOnlyMode],\n+)\n+```\n+\n+The iframe URL is derived during render via `useMemo`, not stored in state and synced with `useEffect`. The component has about 8 props, but they describe one concept: browser session display.\n+\n+## Allowed-Shallow Examples\n+\n+### Button - design system leaf\n+\n+Rule: Allowed-shallow exception - design system leaf\n+\n+Source: `~/Code/toyo` UI primitives\n+\n+```tsx\n+function Button({\n+  className,\n+  variant = \"default\",\n+  size = \"default\",\n+  asChild = false,\n+  ...props\n+}: ButtonProps) {\n+  const Comp = asChild ? Slot.Root : \"button\"\n+  return (\n+    <Comp\n+      data-slot=\"button\"\n+      className={cn(buttonVariants({ variant, size, className }))}\n+      {...props}\n+    />\n+  )\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Zero logic. CVA handles variants, Slot handles polymorphism, and the rest spread passes button attributes through. This is intentionally shallow. Its job is visual consistency, not workflow ownership.\n+\n+### Card + CardHeader - compound component pattern\n+\n+Rule: Composition over Configuration, design system side\n+\n+Source: `~/Code/toyo` UI primitives\n+\n+```tsx\n+function Card({ className, ...props }: React.ComponentProps<\"div\">) {\n+  return (\n+    <div\n+      data-slot=\"card\"\n+      className={cn(\"bg-card text-card-foreground flex flex-col gap-6 rounded-xl border py-6 shadow-sm\", className)}\n+      {...props}\n+    />\n+  )\n+}\n+\n+function CardHeader({ className, ...props }: React.ComponentProps<\"div\">) {\n+  return (\n+    <div\n+      data-slot=\"card-header\"\n+      className={cn(\"@container/card-header grid auto-rows-min ...\", className)}\n+      {...props}\n+    />\n+  )\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Each sub-component is a thin wrapper. Callers compose: `<Card><CardHeader>...</CardHeader><CardContent>...</CardContent></Card>`. No config objects.\n+\n+## Hook Pattern - Derive Don't Sync\n+\n+### useChatScroll - pure function derives from DOM\n+\n+Rule: Derive Don't Sync\n+\n+Source: `~/Code/toyo` chat scroll behavior\n+\n+```typescript\n+export function isAtBottomPosition(\n+  element: Pick<HTMLDivElement, \"scrollHeight\" | \"scrollTop\" | \"clientHeight\">\n+): boolean {\n+  return element.scrollHeight - element.scrollTop - element.clientHeight < AT_BOTTOM_THRESHOLD\n+}\n+\n+// Inside useChatScroll:\n+function onScroll() {\n+  const el = scrollRef.current\n+  if (!el) return\n+  const atBottom = isAtBottomPosition(el)\n+  isBottomLockedRef.current = getNextBottomLockState({\n+    atBottom,\n+    nextScrollTop: el.scrollTop,\n+    previousScrollTop: previousScrollTopRef.current,\n+    wasLocked: isBottomLockedRef.current,\n+    isUserScrollUp: recentUserScrollIntentRef.current === \"up\",\n+  })\n+  setIsAtBottom(atBottom)\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Pure function computes `isAtBottom` from DOM measurements. Lock state lives in a ref, not `useState`. Display state, `isAtBottom`, is the only `useState`; it is the minimal state needed for rendering.\n+\n+## Anti-Pattern with Fix\n+\n+### Anti-pattern: useEffect syncing derived state\n+\n+```typescript\n+// BAD: syncing a pure derivation via useEffect\n+const [isAtBottom, setIsAtBottom] = useState(false)\n+\n+useEffect(() => {\n+  setIsAtBottom(\n+    scrollRef.current.scrollHeight -\n+    scrollRef.current.scrollTop -\n+    scrollRef.current.clientHeight < 50\n+  )\n+}, [scrollRef.current?.scrollHeight])\n+```\n+\n+Why it is wrong: this derives state in an effect with incomplete dependency tracking. It creates stale closures, triggers unnecessary renders, and races with scroll events.\n+\n+Fix: extract the pure function and call it in the scroll handler.\n+\n+```typescript\n+// GOOD: pure function called on demand\n+const atBottom = isAtBottomPosition(scrollRef.current)\n+setIsAtBottom(atBottom)\n+```\ndiff --git a/skills/frontend/TESTING.md b/skills/frontend/TESTING.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..1386fdb\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/skills/frontend/TESTING.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,73 @@\n+# Frontend Testing\n+\n+## Core Principle\n+\n+Test what users see and do, not how the component works internally.\n+\n+Frontend tests should read like behavior specs. A good test says a user can open a menu, submit a form, see an error, or trigger a semantic callback. It should not care whether the component uses local state, a hook, a reducer, or extracted child components.\n+\n+## How to Test Components\n+\n+Render the component with realistic props.\n+\n+```tsx\n+render(<FilesDrawer open onClose={onClose} mode=\"drawer\" />)\n+```\n+\n+Query by role, label, or visible text.\n+\n+```tsx\n+screen.getByRole(\"button\", { name: /new folder/i })\n+screen.getByLabelText(/file name/i)\n+screen.getByText(/uploads/i)\n+```\n+\n+Use `userEvent` for real interaction. Prefer `click`, `type`, and `tab` over `fireEvent`.\n+\n+```tsx\n+const user = userEvent.setup()\n+\n+await user.click(screen.getByRole(\"button\", { name: /new folder/i }))\n+await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/folder name/i), \"Reports\")\n+await user.click(screen.getByRole(\"button\", { name: /create/i }))\n+```\n+\n+Assert visible outcomes and semantic events.\n+\n+```tsx\n+expect(screen.getByText(\"Reports\")).toBeVisible()\n+expect(onClose).not.toHaveBeenCalled()\n+```\n+\n+## What Not to Test\n+\n+Do not test prop plumbing. A test that only proves a parent passes `foo` to a child will break when the component is deepened without proving behavior changed.\n+\n+Do not test hook calls. Avoid assertions such as \"useEffect fired\" or \"useState was set.\" Test the rendered result or semantic callback instead.\n+\n+Do not test internal state names or values. State is an implementation detail.\n+\n+Do not test class names or Tailwind utility classes. Use visual verification for layout and styling behavior.\n+\n+Do not use snapshot tests for layout. Snapshots are noisy for frontend structure and weak at proving what users can see or do.\n+\n+## Hook Logic Boundary\n+\n+Complex hook logic belongs in separate `/tdd` tasks when it has validation, state machines, data transformation, or other behavior that can be tested through a non-rendering interface.\n+\n+Component tests should not re-test hook internals. They should test the visible behavior that results from the hook.\n+\n+For example, a hook test can prove the state machine chooses the next chat scroll lock state. A component test should prove the scroll control appears, disappears, or responds to user action in the rendered UI.\n+\n+## Test Survival Rule\n+\n+A good test survives refactoring that preserves behavior.\n+\n+If renaming internal state, extracting a sub-component, or switching from local state to a hook breaks the test, the test was coupled to implementation.\n+\n+Before keeping a component test, ask:\n+\n+- Would this still pass if the component had different private state names?\n+- Would this still pass if part of the UI moved into a child component?\n+- Would this still pass if state moved from `useState` to a tested hook?\n+- Does this fail when a user-visible behavior actually breaks?\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
